    "recommendations": "Reducing direct vegetation contact with structures and creating greater horizontal separation between shrub masses, tree clusters, and building perimeters could meaningfully improve defensible space and reduce fuel continuity across the property. Where brush and other vegetation are observed directly adjacent to structures, thinning or removing those fuels within the immediate structure zone would reduce the opportunity for surface fire to reach building surfaces. Managing the mixed and scattered vegetation across open terrain to increase spacing between individual plants and clusters may further reduce fuel continuity. Trimming lower limbs on trees within clusters could help separate surface and canopy fuels. Clearing and maintaining vegetation along the access route would support firefighter access and suppression operations during an incident. The existing irrigated landscape near structures represents a valuable fuel break that should be preserved and expanded where feasible, as maintained irrigated zones could improve defensible space and contribute to long-term wildfire resilience for the property."
